<p>John Coppinger Ashlin, was years later to reside at Castleredmond House, and followed in his father’s profession. Amongst others; many of his activities are reported within the “Midleton Chronicles”. Ashlin Street, Midleton may well be named in his honour, or at least his family.</p>
<p>Reverend Stephen Coppinger above, and brother of Dorinda, brought the Presentation Sisters to Midleton after he had built their convent and school. Bishop William, their uncle, died, 11<sup>th</sup> August 1830. Another brother of Dorinda and Rev Stephen, Thomas Stephen Coppinger (Midleton Clan) married, 1840, Annette Power. Stephen Coppinger Ashlin above became a priest, and is known to have served as Curate, Queenstown, now Cobh, 1861. He would later serve as administrator for Bishop Keane at the Cathedral.</p>
<p>When Pugin received the commission for SS Peter and Paul, Cork City, in 1859, he made Ashlin a partner with responsibility for their Irish work until late 1868. In 1861 they opened their office’s of Pugin &amp; Ashlin, 90 St. Stephen’s Green, Dublin. Their practice was primarily ecclesiastical; designing some 25 religious buildings; including the Augustinian church of SS Augustine and John, Thomas Street, Dublin; commissioned in 1860 and opened in 1874. Their churches and Cathedrals were mainly in counties; Wexford, Cork and Kerry, and are all of similar design. By far the most important commission undertaken by this partnership was the building of Cobh’s, St Coleman’s Cathedral (1869-1919) for Bishop William Keane. Others include churches of; Listowel (1865), Brosna (1866), Tralee (1866), Tralee Cathedral (1868), Ardfert (1868), Clonakilty (1866), Fermoy (1867), Monkstown (1867), Ballyhooley (1867), Skibbereen (1867), Crosshaven (1868), Ballynoe (1869). Additionally,the Cathedrals of Skibbereen and Killarney which were began in 1867 during their partnership and completed 1883.</p>
<figure class="figure figure--right">
  <img src="{{ '/assets/images/historical/ashlin/carrigtwohill-church.jpg' | url }}" alt="Carrigtwohill Catholic Parish Church">
  <figcaption>Carrigtwohill Catholic Parish Church</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>By 1872, George Coppinger Ashlin designed his own Parish Church at Carrigtwohill, which was consecrated 1875 by, Bishop William Keane.</p>
<p>The partnership was dissolved in 1870. Thereafter George Coppinger Ashlin practised in his own right, which became nationwide. Amongst his latter works were; Ballycotten (1901), Ballybunion (1892), Holy Rosary Church (1893) and the Munster &amp; Leinster Bank, both of Midleton, and finally Kildare (1898).</p>
<p>George, married Pugin’s sister, Mary in 1867. Their only daughter, Miriam was born ten years later. She married Stephen Musson Ashlin, who joined the family Architect’s business until his death 1942. George Coppinger Ashlin died 10<sup>th</sup> December 1921, and laid to rest, Glasnavin, Dublin</p>
